{"y":1981,"label":"1980–81","coach":"Dom Perno","conf":"Big East","confShort":"Big East","w":20,"l":9,"cw":8,"cl":6,"confFinish":"5th in Big East","finish":"nit","apHigh":20,"srs":5.9,"sos":0.31,"ortg":100.4,"drtg":91.4,"ppg":69.6,"oppg":63.3,"story":{"headline":"Eleven Straight Wins and a Top-20 Ranking","text":"With Chuck Aleksinas, a 6-foot-11 transfer from Kentucky, joining juniors Corny Thompson and Mike McKay, the 1980–81 Huskies won their first 11 games, a run that included a 78–59 win over Syracuse, and entered the AP poll at No. 20 on January 19. The first loss came by a point, 58–57 at Boston College, but UConn kept beating Big East rivals: 69–68 over St. John's in Hartford, 75–73 in overtime over Georgetown, and 65–63 at Syracuse in the new Carrier Dome. The Huskies went 8–6 in the league, lost to Villanova 65–54 in the Big East tournament quarterfinals in Syracuse, then reached the NIT for a second straight year. They won 65–55 at South Florida before Minnesota ended the season 84–66 at the Hartford Civic Center. Thompson (15.0 points, 8.1 rebounds) made first-team All-Big East, Aleksinas (13.8 points on .631 shooting) second team, McKay third team and freshman guard Vern Giscombe the all-freshman team, while freshman point guard Karl Hobbs averaged 4.7 assists. At 20–9, it was UConn's third straight 20-win season.","moments":[{"date":"1981-01-12","title":"11–0","text":"A 61–58 win at New Hampshire makes UConn 11–0; a week later the Huskies are ranked No. 20 in the AP poll."},{"date":"1981-01-24","title":"Overtime over Georgetown","text":"UConn beats Georgetown 75–73 in overtime."},{"date":"1981-02-14","title":"Win at the Carrier Dome","text":"UConn wins 65–63 at Syracuse in the Carrier Dome's first season."},{"date":"1981-03-12","title":"NIT First Round: W at South Florida","text":"UConn wins 65–55 at South Florida in the first round of the NIT."}],"honors":["Corny Thompson – First-team All-Big East","Chuck Aleksinas – Second-team All-Big East","Mike Mc